cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Sluicer: deterministic extraction of the data a web page declares"
message: "If you use Sluicer in work you publish, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Briculschi
    given-names: Nichita
repository-code: "https://github.com/Gi0tto/sluicer"
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-SA-3.0
  - Unicode-3.0
version: 0.8.0
date-released: "2026-09-25"
abstract: >-
  Sluicer reads the structured data a web page already declares, in JSON-LD,
  microdata, RDFa, Dublin Core, OpenGraph, the Twitter card, HTML's own metadata
  names and, on request, microformats2, and returns records in which every field
  carries the vocabulary that produced it. It calls no language model and requires no paid
  API, so the same page always yields the same answer. It fetches at the lowest
  cost that works, climbing to a browser only on an explicit measurement, and it
  announces itself by name and obeys robots.txt.
keywords:
  - web scraping
  - structured data
  - schema.org
  - information extraction
  - reproducibility
